.SH DESCRIPTION
.B omn
describes a measurement along five axes and refuses the combinations that cannot
mean anything, rather than answering them with a number that means something
else.
.PP
It speaks to
.BR perf_event_open (2),
.BR bpf (2),
procfs, sysfs and netlink directly. It does not wrap
.BR perf (1),
.B bpftrace
or
.BR bcc ,
so there is no second output format to parse and no second clock in the middle
of the pipeline.
.SS The five axes
.TP
.B source
What to measure, as
.IR family . metric :
.BR io.latency ", " cpu.instructions ", " net.tcp.rtt .
.BR "omn list" " enumerates them."
Several, separated by commas or spaces, are measured over one window and one
launch, each with the same target and axes:
.B "omn trace cpu.instructions,cpu.cycles \-\- prog"
is
.BR "perf stat \-e instructions,cycles \-\- prog" .
A family alone is the lazy mode (below).
.TP
.B target
What to observe:
.BR \-\-pid ", " \-\-tid ", " \-\-cgroup ", " \-\-cpu ", " \-\-iface ", "
.BR \-\-container ", " \-\-system ", or a program to launch after " \-\- .
.TP
.B mode
How to observe:
.BR count ", " sample@997hz ", " trace ", " poll@100ms ", " snapshot .
.TP
.B emit
What comes out:
.BR raw ", " hist ", " rate ", " topk ", " stack ", " timeline ", " summary .
.TP
.B keys
Correlation keys to attach:
.BR req ", " task ", " proc ", " file ", " sock ", " blk ", " mem ", " gpu ", "
.BR cgroup ", " iface ", " dev ", " cpu ", " node ", " dir ", " syscall ", "
.BR comm ", " domain ", " func ", " op ", " level ", " mapping ", " user ", "
.BR counter ", " within ", " insn ", " cache ", " object .
.B dir
splits a byte or request counter by direction,
.B syscall
and
.B comm
key the syscall sources by call and by task name,
.B domain
splits
.B power.energy
by RAPL domain, and
.B func
is derived from each event's stack (its leaf frame), not attached by a
collector; a source offers it by listing it in its catalog
.BR keys: .
.B within
is every function on the stack, the inclusive share
.RB ( "perf report \-\-children" ),
the one key that counts an event in several series;
.B insn
the leaf's instruction and
.B object
the file it is in
.RB ( "perf report \-\-sort dso" ).
.BR op ", " level ", " mapping ", " user ", " counter " and " cache
are read off the event attribute of that name: an operation, a memory level,
a mapping
.RB ( "pmap \-X" ),
a user
.RB ( top 's
USER), a counter
.RB ( nstat ),
a slab cache
.RB ( slabtop ).
.PP
A bare
.BI "omn " source
is short for
.BI "omn trace " source .
.SS A family alone: the lazy mode
A family name where a source is expected \(em
.BR "omn cpu \-\- prog" ,
.BR "omn trace mem \-\-pid 1234 \-d 5s" ,
.B "omn io \-\-cost med \-\- prog"
\(em composes that family's default: the minimum that answers its first
question, written in the family's own catalog file as data, one list of
requests per cost band. A family name has no dot, so it never collides with a
source; a word that is neither is refused naming the families.
.BI \-\-cost " low" "\fR|\fP" med "\fR|\fP" high
picks the band: absent, the cheapest the family declares; a band it does not
declare falls to the dearest under it, and says so; one under its cheapest is
refused with the band to ask. The person gives only the target,
.B \-\-duration
and
.BR \-\-budget ;
the per-request flags
.RB ( \-\-mode ", " \-\-emit ", " \-\-keys ", " \-\-filter ", " \-\-stack ", "
.BR \-\-symbols ", " \-\-backend ", " \-\-window ", " \-\-until ", " \-\-label )
are refused by name, and
.B \-\-cost
beside a source or a session file points at
.BR \-\-budget .
.PP
Before anything is resolved, the run prints the band, the family's question
and every request it composed in the long form, which typed back asks for the
same requests; after resolution it names each request this machine serves
through a backend that declares more than the band. Then it says what it
chose, degraded and lost like any run.
.B \-\-cost
chooses requests and is not a ceiling: the measured ceiling stays
.BR \-\-budget 's.
A family with no safe default
.RB ( runtime )
is refused with the reason and its sources; a machine-wide one
.RB ( power )
runs on
.B \-\-system
with a duration, never on a program.
.B omn list
ends with every family's default.
.SS What it refuses
The validator runs before anything is attached. It rejects a target a source
cannot carry, a mode it does not support, an emit that mode cannot produce, a
correlation key it does not carry, a filter field it does not expose, and an
output format that cannot express the requested emit. Each refusal says why and
what the source does accept.
.PP
Returning a machine-wide value while presenting it as per-process attribution is
the worst bug this tool could have, so the combination fails rather than
degrading.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "trace " source
Build a request, resolve it against this machine, and run it. The default
command, which may be omitted.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fIfamily\fR|\fIsource\fR]
Project the catalog. With no argument, every source; with a family, that family;
with a source, that entry in full, including every backend, what each one
requires and how each requirement was probed.
.IP
The listing answers three different questions in three columns.
.B HERE
is what this machine offers, probed now.
.B COLLECTOR
is what this build can measure, which is not the same question.
.B COST
is the cheapest backend in a chain, and
.B DET
is reproducibility between runs \(em never bit-exactness.
.IP
.B \-\-verify\-attach
answers every raw\-tracepoint requirement by attaching to it rather than by
reading the kernel's BTF. Those are not the same question: BTF says the kernel
defines a tracepoint, and the attach is what a run will actually do. They have
never yet disagreed, which the suite checks rather than assumes, and asking the
expensive one of every entry costs about a second \(em so it is a switch and not
the default. Reach for it on a machine where a backend probes available and then
refuses once a target is running.
.IP
.B \-\-defaults
lists each family's default instead of the sources \(em what
.B omn trace
.I family
composes, per cost band, or why the family has none \(em and with
.B \-\-format json
as one document whose requests are a session's once a target is added.
.TP
.B validate \fB\-f\fI session
Check a session against the catalog without touching the machine. Machine
independent, so it answers for a host other than this one.
.TP
.B formats
The input and output formats, which emits each output format can carry, and
which encoders exist yet.
.TP
.B latency \-\-objective \fIobjective\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIprogram\fR
Why is this slow. Thread state analysis, asked as a question: one task's wall
clock decomposed into on a cpu, runnable and blocked, the blocked time split by
the task state each block recorded, and the state that dominates named with
what covers it \(em a pthread mutex, a futex, the stack a block began from, the
hottest function. See
.BR TRIAGE .
.IP
.BI \-\-objective " latency" "\fR|\fP" tail
is required. \fBlatency\fR decomposes the median run; \fBtail\fR decomposes
the slowest run against the median and names the state it spent more time in.
.BI \-\-runs " N"
runs a launched program \fIN\fR times (default 1; the tail needs 3 or more).
.BI \-\-pid " PID"
decomposes a process already running over \fB\-\-duration\fR, which it then
requires; its whole is the window rather than a lifetime, and it has no tail.
.BI \-\-hz " HZ"
is the profile's rate (default 997), whose period is the on-cpu row's
resolution.
.BI \-\-symbols " SOURCE"
is where the stacks' names come from, as for a single source: set on every
request of the question whose collector reads it (\fBcpu.profile\fR in this
build), and on no other, so that \fBdebuginfod\fR names a stripped binary's
hottest function rather than ranking it by instruction.
.TP
.B saturate \-\-objective \fIobjective\fR [\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fIprogram\fR
What does this queue for. The USE method \(em utilization, saturation and
errors per resource: cpu, disk, lock, memory \(em with Little's law as its
arithmetic.
.BI \-\-objective " throughput" "\fR|\fP" tail "\fR|\fP" energy
is required. See
.BR TRIAGE .
.BI \-\-symbols " SOURCE"
is accepted as \fBomn latency\fR's is, and set on every request whose
collector reads it; the USE method names resources, not functions, so none of
its requests reads it today, and the answer says so when it is given.
.TP
.B diff \fIbefore after\fR
Two profiles and a subtraction. Each is a run's output directory (written with
.B \-o
and
.B \-\-format json
or
.BR folded ),
its
.IR results.json ,
or a folded file. Names the functions whose own cost moved beyond the noise a
sample count has, compared in samples at one rate and in time at two.
.B \-\-format folded
writes the three-column
.I "stack before after"
file differential flame graphs are drawn from;
.BI \-\-top " N"
bounds each table.
.IP
The three triage commands take
.B \-\-format
text, json or yaml, and
.B \-\-dump\-session
prints the session the question is asked through and exits: an ordinary
session, which
.B omn \-f
runs to print the tables the answer is read from.
.TP
.B join \-f \fIrules results\fR
Applies join rules to a stored run: the query tier, after the run.
.I rules
is a session file's \fBjoins:\fR, a list of rules or one rule, in YAML or
JSON; every rule in it is applied, whatever its \fBtier:\fR says.
.I results
is a run's \fIresults.json\fR or the directory it was written to with
.BR \-o ,
and each side of a rule must have been stored with \fB\-e raw\fR. Prints the
correlations as a run prints its joins, in
.B \-\-format
text, json, yaml or toml. A rule the stored run cannot answer -- a side with no
result, or one stored without \fBraw\fR -- refuses the command with exit 2
rather than being left out.
.TP
.B import \fItrace results\fR [\fB\-o\fR \fIfile\fR]
Merges a CTF 1.8 trace, as LTTng writes one, into a stored run, so
.B omn join
meets its events with the run's. An import rather than a source: LTTng-UST
writes in-line into its own buffer, so its events exist for no program of
.BR omn 's.
.I trace
is a directory holding \fImetadata\fR and its data streams, or one above
them, as LTTng writes a session;
.I results
is a run's \fIresults.json\fR or its
.B \-o
directory. Each event becomes one of source \fBctf.event\fR, stored
\fBraw\fR: \fBevent\fR is its name, its fields are its attributes (a
leading underscore taken off), and \fBtask\fR, \fBproc\fR and \fBcpu\fR
are its \fBvtid\fR, \fBvpid\fR and packet's cpu -- the traced process's
own ids, which inside a container are not the host's. Only LTTng's
\fBmonotonic\fR clock is accepted, because it is CLOCK_MONOTONIC; an event
outside the run's window is counted and left out, a trace none of whose events
falls inside it is refused, and the events the tracer discarded are the
result's losses. Writes the merged run as JSON to
.I file
or standard output, and says what it kept on standard error.
.TP
.B top \fR[\fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-\-pid\fR \fIpid\fR | \fB\-\-\fR \fIprogram\fR]
An interactive terminal view of what
.B omn
measures, live or from a recording. See
.BR "THE VIEW" .
.TP
.B selftest \fR[\fB\-\-format\fR text|json] [\fB\-\-sources\fR \fIsource,...\fR] [\fB\-d\fR \fIduration\fR]
What this machine serves. Every source is listed, as
.B omn list
answers it with its reasons, and run for
.I duration
(300ms by default) on a launched program and on the machine, by this same
binary as a separate program, so that the answer is the one a user meets
with the capabilities the binary carries. Prints one line a source -- usable
or not here, and for each target whether it attached or why not -- and
counts the sources listed usable that were refused at start, which should be
none.
.B \-\-format json
writes the portability matrix's cell, which
.B scripts/matrix.sh
and distroproof's guests record and the suite's judges read
(\fIdocs/portability.md\fR, \fIdocs/distroproof.md\fR);
.B \-\-cell
and
.B \-\-config
name it,
.B \-\-fixtures
points at a checkout's \fItarget/\fR for the program-probe sources' fixtures,
and
.B \-\-strict
exits 1 when a source listed usable is refused at start. A full sweep takes
about two minutes.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Target
.TP
.BI \-\-pid " PID" "\fR, " \-\-tid " TID"
An existing process or thread. Repeatable as a comma-separated list. A run on
.B \-\-pid
ends when its process exits (with several, when the last does), as
.B "perf stat \-p"
ends, so
.B \-d
is a ceiling rather than a requirement and
.B "\-m count \-\-pid"
runs without it; a tid is not watched. A run on a pid, tid, cgroup or
container says how long after
.B omn
started each source attached: what the target did before that is in no
reading.
.TP
.BI \-\-cgroup " PATH"
A cgroup v2 path.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " SET"
Cores, written the way the kernel writes them:
.BR 0\-3,8 .
.TP
.BI \-\-iface " NAME"
A network interface.
.TP
.BI \-\-container " NAME"
A container, measured as the cgroup it runs in. The runtime is asked for the
container's init process
.RB ( "docker inspect \-\-format '{{.State.Pid}}'" " NAME, then " podman ,
.BR nerdctl ),
and that process's
.I /proc/<pid>/cgroup
names the cgroup; the run says which it found. A name no runtime knows, or a
stopped container, is refused before anything is attached.
.TP
.B \-\-system
The whole machine.
.TP
.BI \-\- " program " [ args ]
Launch the program and measure it. The program is held at a gate until every
collector is attached, so the measurement starts before its first instruction,
and the run ends when it does. The launcher searches
.B PATH
itself, so the program's one
.BR execve (2)
is the file it found.
.IP
Beside another target \(em
.BR \-\-system ", " \-\-cpu ", " \-\-cgroup ", " \-\-iface ", " \-\-pid ", "
.BR \-\-tid " or " \-\-container
\(em the program is the window, not a second target: that target is measured
for as long as the program runs, the way
.B "perf stat \-a \-\- prog"
and
.B "turbostat prog"
measure. In a session file the window is
.B launch:
beside the requests, and a request that launches a program of its own beside
it is refused: a run launches one program. A source that cannot follow a
program names the bounded spelling in its refusal.
.IP
The program's exit is in
.IR results.json :
.I lifetime.exit_code
when it exited,
.I lifetime.signal
when it was killed.
.B omn
itself exits 0 when the run succeeded, whatever the program returned.
.IP
When the run stops first \(em its duration elapsed, a budget ended it, the
view was quit \(em the program and every process it started are stopped
together, then killed and reaped, and the results say so
.RI ( lifetime.terminated ).
A process the program started that left its tree before the stop \(em
orphaned, its parent gone first, or in a session of its own \(em is not lost:
.B omn
is the subreaper of everything the program starts, so such a process is
adopted by
.B omn
rather than by init, and is ended at the end of the run, whether the program
was stopped or exited on its own leaving it behind. Their count is
.I lifetime.adopted_ended
and is said on standard error. One the stop could not signal (a setuid child
refusing this process's signals) is still running when
.B omn
exits; its count is
.I lifetime.left_running
and is said as a warning.
.TP
.BI \-\-program\-stderr " FILE"
Write the launched program's standard error to
.IR FILE ,
created or truncated, instead of the standard error it would share with
.BR omn ,
so the program's lines and
.BR omn 's
are told apart at the source. What
.B OMN_PROGRAM_STDERR
sets, and it wins over the variable. With
.BI "omn latency \-\-runs " N\fR,
each launch's stderr is also kept as
.IR FILE .1
\&...
.IR FILE . N ,
numbered in the order the launches ran, the file itself holding the last
one's; a launch that wrote nothing takes no number. Refused, with exit 2, beside a command
that launches nothing; a file that cannot be opened stops the launch with the
reason.
.TP
.BI \-\-program\-stdout " FILE"
Write the launched program's standard output to
.IR FILE ,
created or truncated, instead of the standard output it would share with
.BR omn ,
so that
.BR omn 's
standard output holds its document alone:
.B "omn trace cpu.cycles \-m count \-e summary \-\-format yaml \-\-program\-stdout ls.out \-\- ls \-R | yq"
parses. What
.B OMN_PROGRAM_STDOUT
sets, and it wins over the variable. Naming the same file as
.B \-\-program\-stderr
writes both into it, in the order the program wrote them. With several
launches the file holds the last one's. Refused, with exit 2, beside a command
that launches nothing.
.SS Measurement
.TP
.BI \-m ", " \-\-mode " MODE"
.BR count ", " trace ", " snapshot ", " sample@\fIFREQ\fR ", " poll@\fIINTERVAL\fR.
A frequency is
.BR 997hz " or " 1khz ;
an interval is
.BR 100ms ", " 1s ", " 2m .
.TP
.BI \-e ", " \-\-emit " EMIT"
Comma-separated.
.BR hist " takes " :log2 " or " :linear: \fIWIDTH\fR,
a width above zero, and
.BR topk " takes " : \fIN\fR,
or
.BI topk: N @ ATTR
to rank the distinct values of one attribute rather than the series:
.B topk:5@reason
ranks the reasons packets were dropped. A counted window ranks too, and a
ranking that measured a window and counted nothing holds one zero row named
by the target.
.BI summary@ ATTR
and
.BI hist "\fR[\fP" :SCALE "\fR]\fP" @ ATTR
summarise per value of one attribute the same way
.RB ( "summary@kernel" ),
rows named
.IB ATTR = value
and
.IB ATTR =?
for the events that carry none, said; the attribute must be a filter field
the resolved backend fills, or the request is refused before the run.
.B summary
carries a
.B total
where the source's values add up (counts, bytes, times), marked
.B >=
when the run lost records, and none over a level (a resident size, a ratio, a
temperature).
Asking for two emits costs one collection.
.TP
.BI \-k ", " \-\-keys " KEY"
Correlation keys to attach, comma-separated.
.TP
.BI \-\-value " ATTR"
Reduce that attribute instead of the collector's own value, in the unit its
name says; said in the degradations.
.TP
.BI \-\-sum " ATTR[,ATTR]"
Beside a
.BR summary ,
add up the named attributes per series:
.B "proc.syscall \-k syscall \-e summary \-\-sum errors"
is
.BR "strace \-c" 's
table with its errors column.
.TP
.BI \-\-filter " EXPR"
A predicate:
.RB ` "bytes > 4KiB && rwbs == 'R'" '.
Supports
.BR == " " != " " < " " <= " " > " " >= " " =~ " and " in ,
grouped with
.BR && ", " || ", " ! " and parentheses."
Integers take size suffixes
.RB ( 4KiB ", " 4KB )
and duration suffixes
.RB ( 5ms ).
.IP
.BR =~
is a substring test rather than a regular expression:
.RB ` "comm =~ 'python'" '
keeps
.BR python3 .
A pattern that can only be a regex \(em a character class, a group, an escape,
or an anchor \(em is refused rather than matched literally.
.IP
The resolver pushes a predicate into the kernel where the backend allows it and
evaluates it here otherwise, and says which it did. A userspace filter is paid
for in full before it removes anything, and what it removed is reported beside
the losses.
.TP
.BI \-d ", " \-\-duration " DURATION"
How long to observe. Required unless a program is launched, a
.B \-\-pid
is watched, or the mode is
.BR snapshot .
.TP
.BI \-r ", " \-\-repeat " N"
Run the session
.I N
times, one after another, each with a launch of its own, and report each
.B summary
value's mean, sample standard deviation, min, max and relative error of the
mean, as
.B "perf stat \-r"
does. A launched program's
.BI task: pid
keys become
.BI task:# k\fR,
ranked by pid within each run, so the runs line up. Each run's losses are said
on standard error under its
.BI "run " i " of " N
line, and a spread with a lossy run in it names that run. A structured format
prints the spread and every run's output; under
.BR \-o ,
each run is written to
.IR run\-<i>/
and the spread to
.IR repeat.json .
Refused before anything runs: no
.B summary
to spread,
.BR "\-r 0" ,
a recording, a binary format.
.TP
.BI \-\-cost " BAND"
Beside a family, the cost band of its default:
.BR low ", " med " or " high .
See
.BR "A family alone" .
.TP
.BI \-b ", " \-\-budget " PERCENT"
An overhead ceiling, as
.BR 2% ,
read as a percentage of the observed workload's own CPU time. The run weighs
itself ten times a second against that; over the ceiling it moves to a cheaper
backend in the chain, then from
.B trace
to
.B sample
(the same backends, each event then standing for its period, and only on a
collector whose sampled events carry that period as their
.BR weight ;
none does yet, so that step is named as passed over), or, where there is
nothing cheaper, stops. Either way the reason is in
.I stopped_because
and on standard error. Every run reports what it cost, budget or no budget.
.IP
A series moved from one mode to another is one series only to an emit that
weighs each event by what it stands for:
.BR hist ,
.BR topk ,
.B rate
and
.BR summary .
A mode that cannot produce an emit the request asked for is not a step the
budget takes. A budget that could move a request whose
.BR raw ,
.B timeline
or
.B stack
the cheaper mode would produce, on a collector the budget could move it onto,
is refused before the run (exit 2), because that emit would hold two kinds of
event as one.
.IP
What is weighed is this tool's CPU and what the collector spent inside the
target, where the tool's own account cannot see it: an eBPF program's run time
as the kernel counts it, the preload shim's time as the shim publishes it. The
kernel counts a program's run time only while
.B kernel.bpf_stats_enabled
is 1 or a process holding
.B CAP_SYS_ADMIN
asks it to; otherwise that part is
.IR unknown ,
said so, and never taken as zero, and a ceiling over it is reported as not
checked whole rather than held.
.TP
.BI \-\-window " START..STOP"
When, inside the run, the request is attached and detached, relative to the
launch gate:
.BR 1s..3s ,
.BR 1s..
(attached late, kept to the end) or
.BR ..2s
(attached with the rest, detached early). Absent is the whole run. The same
text a session file's
.B window
takes, parsed by the same code, so the two refuse the same things in the same
words. A collection's
.I observed
span and its rates are over its window, and a late attach is named in its notes
with what the attach itself took while the program ran.
.TP
.BI \-\-until " EXPR"
Record until a predicate fires, as a session's
.B record:
block does on its one request: the run keeps only what
.B \-\-retain
holds, and writes one document each time
.I EXPR
\(em the filter grammar, over one round of the request \(em becomes true.
.B value
in it is the request's measured value. Needs
.BR \-\-retain ", " \-\-documents " and " \-\-duration ,
each refused when absent. \fBRECORDING\fR has the rest.
.TP
.BI \-\-retain " DURATION"
How far back a recorded document reaches from its trigger.
.TP
.BI \-\-after " DURATION"
How long a recorded document goes on after its trigger before it is written;
zero when absent.
.TP
.BI \-\-documents " COUNT"
The most documents a recording writes. The run ends with the last.
.TP
.BI \-\-stack " MODE"
.BR auto " (default), " fp ", " fp+leaf ", " dwarf ", " lbr ", " sframe " or " none .
.B auto
takes the first mode of the unwind table this machine and this target serve,
and the resolved file says which and why.
.B dwarf
copies the stack on every sample: far more volume and cost than frame pointers.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbols " SOURCE"
Where stack names come from:
.B local
(default), the files the target mapped, or
.BR debuginfod :
those first, then the servers in
.B DEBUGINFOD_URLS
for a stripped file, asked by its build id and kept in the debuginfod cache.
A request about what this machine runs, so never made unless asked for.
Read by
.B cpu.profile
in this build; on any other source it is refused with exit code 3.
In a session file, a request's
.BR symbols: .
.SS Output
.TP
.BI \-\-format " FORMAT"
.BR text " (default), " json ", " yaml ", " toml ", " ndjson ", " folded ", "
.BR speedscope ", " pprof ", " chrome ", " pcap .
.B pcap
writes the frames
.B net.capture
copied (their headers only), with the kernel's own stamp, for
.BR "tcpdump \-r" .
A result format only accepts the emits it can express;
.B omn formats
says which, and which encoders exist.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-out " DIR"
Write the results and the resolved request into a directory. The resolved
request is always written as JSON whatever the results format is: results
without it are numbers whose provenance is gone.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-request
Print the request and how it resolves on this machine, then exit without
measuring.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-resolved
Print the resolved request alone \(em the record to keep beside the numbers.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-resolve
Do not consult this machine. For writing down a session meant to run somewhere
else, where what this machine can do says nothing.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " WHEN"
.BR auto " (default), " always " or " never .
Colour is an accelerator and never the carrier of a fact: everything it
highlights is also written in words.
.SS Session files
.TP
.BI \-f ", " \-\-file " PATH"
Read the session from a file, or from standard input with
.BR \- .
YAML, JSON and TOML are accepted, inferred from the extension.
.TP
.BI \-\-input\-format " FORMAT"
.BR yaml ", " json " or " toml ,
when the extension does not say or the input is standard input.
.SS Other
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " force:ID"
Pin a backend instead of resolving one. For the comparison bench: a result
obtained this way is not the result the tool would have produced, and the record
says so. A session naming one source several times, each pinned to a different
backend, is the self-benchmark: one target, one window, and a table of what each
backend cost and whether they agreed.
.TP
.BI \-\-catalog " DIR"
Load the catalog from a directory instead of the one compiled in. For iterating
on entries without rebuilding.
.SH SESSION FILES
A session file describes exactly what a command line does, and
.B \-\-dump\-request
prints one. The two surfaces cannot express different things, which is what
keeps them from drifting apart.
.PP
.RS
.EX
name: which allocation actually cost memory
duration: 30s
format: text

requests:
  \- label: ranges
    source: mem.heap.alloc
    target: {launch: [./server]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [summary]

  \- label: pages
    source: mem.faults
    target: {launch: [./server]}
    mode: trace
    emit: [rate]

joins:
  \- id: pages\-of\-allocation
    from: mem.heap.alloc
    to: mem.faults
    via: mem
    match: interval
    opens: "op == 'alloc'"
    closes: "op == 'free'"
    window: 60s
.EE
.RE
.PP
Every request in one session is collected over one window. Running them one
after another would measure several different moments and present them as one,
and a join across that would correlate a program with its own past. A session
has at most one \fIworkload\fR target \(em \fBlaunch\fR, \fBpid\fR, \fBtid\fR,
\fBcgroup\fR or \fBcontainer\fR \(em and any number of \fImachine\fR targets
beside it (\fBsystem\fR, \fBcpu\fR, \fBiface\fR): that is the shape of every
confounder check, where the machine's state is read beside the program it might
explain. Only the workload target decides when the run ends.
.PP
A program that bounds machine targets without being one is written
.B "launch: [prog, args]"
beside the requests: the runner launches it, ends the run with it, and binds
it to no request, which is what
.B "omn trace power.energy \-\-system \-\- prog"
dumps with
.BR \-\-dump\-request .
A request that launches a program of its own beside a
.B launch:
is refused.
.SS Windows and the escalation
A request's \fBwindow\fR is when it is watched inside the run: \fB1s..3s\fR,
\fB1s..\fR or \fB..2s\fR, as on the command line. The rest of the session
keeps running across every attach and detach.
.PP
A window written \fBon\fR \fIlayer\fR is one the run decides: the request is
validated, resolved and prepared before the launch like any other, then held,
attached when the session's escalation names that layer from its own cheap
numbers and detached when it names another. The layers are the states of a
task's wall clock: \fBcpu\fR (on a cpu), \fBqueue\fR (runnable, waiting for
one), \fBdisk\fR (blocked uninterruptibly) and \fBlock\fR (blocked
sleeping). A session that holds a request needs an \fBescalate:\fR block with
its objective, and the cheap pass beside it, run for the whole run:
.PP
.RS
.EX
escalate: {objective: latency}
requests:
  \- {label: blocked, source: sched.offcpu, target: &p {launch: [./prog]},
     mode: trace, emit: [summary], keys: [task]}
  \- {label: on\-cpu, source: cpu.oncpu_time, target: *p,
     mode: poll@50ms, emit: [summary]}
  \- {label: profile, source: cpu.profile, target: *p, mode: sample@997hz,
     emit: [stack], stack: fp, window: on cpu}
  \- {label: futex, source: sched.futex, target: *p, mode: trace,
     emit: [summary], window: on lock}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A held request is attached at most once: a request has one window. A join or a
decomposition that reads a held source is refused until the join engine knows
when a source was watching; join the cheap pass instead. \fBTRIAGE\fR says how
the escalation decides.
.SS Recording
A session with a \fBrecord:\fR block is a flight recorder: a run whose
duration is until a predicate fires. \fBRECORDING\fR says what it does.
.PP
.RS
.EX
duration: 30s
requests:
  \- {label: descriptors, source: proc.fd, target: {launch: [./prog]},
     mode: poll@20ms, emit: [summary]}
record:
  trigger: "value >= 150"
  on: descriptors
  retain: 1s
  after: 600ms
  documents: 5
.EE
.RE
.SS Joins
A join is declared beside its sources and matches their events by a key.
.TP
.B interval
An event is credited to the state whose range it falls in: an allocation's
pages to the allocation. Opened and closed by predicates.
.TP
.B eq
Both sides carry the same key. Which of several open states of one key an
event goes to is decided by recency, and is reported as \fBwithin_key:
TEMPORAL\fR beside the \fBKEY\fR, because recency is the clock and not the key.
.TP
.B scoped_eq
The same, on a key whose values are recycled: a request pointer is scoped by
the moment it was opened, \fBblk:\fIptr\fB@\fIopen_ts\fR.
.TP
.B wakeup
A task's run is credited to the wakeup that began it. Refused before the run
onto a \fBdrained\fR source, whose events are sums of blocks.
.TP
.B between
A polled total's change between two readings is credited to the states that
opened or closed in that interval. \fBTEMPORAL\fR by construction.
.PP
A rule's \fBtier:\fR says where it is applied.
.B agent
(the default) applies it in this process when the run ends, over the events
collected.
.B query
applies it after the run, over the stored run's \fBraw\fR output: the run
refuses such a rule unless both of its sides are stored with \fBraw\fR, and
.B omn join
applies it.
.B kernel
is refused with the reason: no match kind has a program in the kernel yet.
.SS Decompositions
A \fBdecompose:\fR block splits a whole into parts, per key:
.PP
.RS
.EX
decompose:
  \- id: where\-the\-time\-went
    whole: lifetime
    parts:
      \- {state: on\-cpu,   from: cpu.profile,   value: samples}
      \- {state: runnable, from: sched.runqlat, value: total_ns}
      \- {state: blocked,  from: sched.offcpu,  value: total_ns, less: queued_ns}
.EE
.RE
.PP
A part reads \fIvalue\fR off each event of its source \(em an attribute, or
\fBsamples\fR for a sampled source, which is the count times the period \(em
and may take \fBless:\fR an attribute to subtract, \fBfilter:\fR a predicate
choosing which events it reads, \fBreading: level\fR|\fBdelta\fR, required for a
polled source, and \fBnames:\fR an attribute whose most frequent value the row
names beside its amount, with how many of its events agreed. \fBby:\fR is the
key each table is read per (\fBtask\fR unless said), and \fBtop:\fR how many
tables to print.
.PP
The whole is \fBwindow\fR (the run, from its start to the last drain: an upper
bound), \fBlifetime\fR (the launched program, from the gate to the reap: exact
at the start, bracketed at the exit, and refused where nothing was launched),
or a measurement read off a source. Each row says what kind of number it is \(em
sampled, polled, traced, counted \(em and its resolution. The residue is
\fIwhole \- parts\fR, signed and never rounded away: negative is something
counted twice, positive is a state no row covers, and one inside its own
resolution is the decomposition holding.
.SH RECORDING
A recording keeps only the last \fBretain\fR of what its requests read;
everything older is evicted, and counted. After every round of the request
named by \fBon\fR (which may be left out when the session has one), the
\fBtrigger\fR is evaluated over the events that round read. It fires on the
round in which it becomes true, and again only after a round in which it was
not, so a level held for a second writes one document and not fifty. When it
fires the run goes on for \fBafter\fR, then cuts a document: the window from
\fBretain\fR before the trigger to that round, every request's events inside
it, and the trigger itself \(em the predicate, the request, the monotonic
time of the round and of the event that fired it, the value it fired with.
Then it goes on recording. It ends at its \fBdocuments\fR, at its
\fBduration\fR, or when a launched program exits; a document still open
then is cut short and says so.
.PP
The trigger reads \fBvalue\fR, \fBkind\fR, and the fields the catalog lets
the source be filtered on through the backend the resolver chose; any other
field is refused before the launch, since it could never fire. The three
bounds are required: without \fBretain\fR the run would keep everything,
without \fBdocuments\fR it could write forever, and without \fBduration\fR
its end would be a trigger that may never fire. A request in \fBcount\fR or
\fBsnapshot\fR mode, a \fBwindow\fR, an \fBescalate:\fR block and a
decomposition are each refused beside a recording, with the reason.
.PP
With \fB\-o\fR \fIDIR\fR each document is \fIDIR\fR/trigger\-\fIN\fR.\fIext\fR,
written the moment it is cut. Without it the documents go to standard output
one after another, which only \fBtext\fR and \fBndjson\fR can carry; any
other format is refused there. Each document counts, per request, what was
evicted since the one before it: read, paid for, and in no document. The
count is a loss, and is on the document's loss line and beside its
distributions \(em without the \fB>=\fR mark, since everything inside the
window is there.
.PP
The budget is weighed over a rolling span rather than the whole run: the
longer of \fBretain\fR and two seconds. A recorder that idled for an hour
has not banked an hour of budget for what it does next.
.PP
\fB\-\-duration\fR on \fB\-f\fR replaces a recording's longest duration,
and the bounds are checked again against it.
.SH EVIDENCE
Every correlated result carries the grounds it was obtained on, because a
correlation backed by a propagated identity and one backed by two things
happening at the same time are both "correlations" and only one is evidence.
.TP
.B IDENTITY
The downstream event carries an identity the upstream one propagated.
.TP
.B KEY
Both sides carry the same typed key.
.TP
.B WAKEUP
A wakeup chain links them: the only join that crosses a block.
.TP
.B TEMPORAL
They overlapped in time. A hypothesis, nothing more. What \fBbetween\fR
produces, and what \fBwithin_key\fR says of an \fBeq\fR join's choice between
states.
.SH TRIAGE
.BR "omn latency" ", " "omn saturate" " and " "omn diff"
sit above the engine and call it like any other user: each writes a session,
runs it through the same calls
.B omn \-f
makes, and reads the answer off the results. What they compute themselves \(em
a share, a Little's-law product, a difference of two profiles \(em is
arithmetic on numbers the engine reported, with every row's evidence,
resolution and shortfall carried through. A value marked \fB>=\fR comes from a
lossy run and is a lower bound: what a stalled machine fails to record is the
slow part.
.SS The objective
"What is this limited by" has no answer until someone says \fIfor what\fR, and a
triage question without \fB\-\-objective\fR is refused with the list rather
than defaulted.
.TP
.B latency
How long a typical run takes: the median run, decomposed.
.TP
.B tail
How long the slowest runs take. For \fBomn latency\fR, what the slowest of
\fB\-\-runs\fR spent that the median did not \(em the maximum of \fIN\fR,
which is not a percentile below a hundred runs, and says so. For \fBomn
saturate\fR, the resource whose individual waits have the longest 99th
percentile, read off each source's histogram and known to its log2 bucket.
.TP
.B throughput
How much work gets done per second: the resource the work queues for. Only
\fBomn saturate\fR answers it; a wall clock does not.
.TP
.B energy
What burns the energy this machine meters. Only \fBomn saturate\fR answers
it: the package's energy is credited to the workload by its share of the
machine's busy cpu time, which is an estimate and is marked \fBTEMPORAL\fR, and
the disk's and the network's energy are named as unmetered rather than free.
.PP
Each method refuses what it cannot answer and names the one that can:
\fBomn latency \-\-objective throughput\fR points at \fBomn saturate\fR, and
\fBomn saturate \-\-objective latency\fR at \fBomn latency\fR.
.SS Verdicts
\fBomn latency\fR names the largest state only when nothing unread could
overtake it: not the next state, not a row the run did not supply, not the
residue and the rows' resolution. Otherwise it says undecided, and why. It
starts from one task \(em a launched program's main thread, or the process
asked for \(em and follows its largest row when that row is a sleep or a
futex wait: into the child that ran during a wait for it (in the wait's
place), or to another thread of the process that ran during the block (beside
it), up to eight deep; the verdict says which task its state is from, and
lists the others. A followed child's row that no source saw is unread, not
zero. \fBsched.mutex\fR is asked in a second pass, only when a futex sleep
needs splitting. A sleeping state is named by the lock
source that covers more than half of it: \fBsched.mutex\fR (\fIlock\fR), then
\fBsched.futex\fR (\fIfutex\fR), else \fIsleep\fR with the stack it began
from; \fIuninterruptible\fR is \fIdisk\fR.
.PP
What a verdict names, and how it knows the name. The hottest function is the
one the task's cpu samples were charged to most: a sample's leaf frame, or,
where a perf map named a frame of its stack, the innermost such frame \(em
under \fBpython3 \-X perf\fR or \fBnode \-\-perf\-basic\-prof\fR the
leaf is always the runtime's, and the program's function is the frame the map
named; the sentence says which. The maps are the ones the profile's names note
cites, read back from \fI/tmp\fR; one that is gone is said. A profile of an
interpreter (python, node, a JVM, ruby, perl, php, lua and their usual
spellings, by a name the program goes by or by its library in at least half
the samples) whose hottest function was charged by its leaf and holds less
than half the samples is said to be spread, with its top C functions listed
without saying whose they are and what would write a perf map; none is
presented as the suspect. A function charged by its leaf while some samples
had a perf-map frame is said to be. A block's stack is printed leaf first to
the program's own frame (the innermost one a perf map named, else the
innermost in its executable, symlinks followed): at least four frames, at
most sixteen. The verdict's notes carry each profile's \fInames came from\fR
line under its request's label.
.PP
A launched program that failed \(em an exit status other than zero, or a
signal it died of rather than the one the run stopped it with \(em is said
under the verdict, as a warning naming the run, and carried as
\fIprogram_failed\fR: a verdict read off a usage error or a crash decomposes a
run nobody asked about, and would otherwise read like one that worked.
\fBomn saturate\fR says the same of its workload.
.PP
\fBomn saturate\fR reads, per resource, the workload's side: cpus busy with it
and tasks waiting for a cpu; requests in flight at the device, tasks blocked on
it, and requests that failed; a mutex's hold time and its waiters; its
cgroup's memory stall share. Every mean count is Little's \fIL\fR, the arrival
rate times the mean wait over the window \(em the sum of the waits over
\fIT\fR, exact over a traced source \(em and printed term by term.
For throughput it names the longest queue behind something another task holds,
a cpu or a lock, when one reaches a tenth of a waiter on average; below that,
nothing is saturated and the workload is limited by its own concurrency, which
is the answer for a program waiting on one synchronous write at a time.
.PP
\fBomn diff\fR compares absolute costs, not shares: when one function doubles,
every other function's share falls without any of them changing. A function
is the difference when its own cost \(em its leaf samples, or under a perf
map the samples whose innermost named frame it is \(em moved beyond three standard
deviations of Poisson noise, \fI|b \- a| / sqrt(a + b)\fR; the inclusive table
shows what carried it. A folded file carries no loss accounting and no rate,
and the answer says it could not check either. A perf map a run cites is read
back from \fI/tmp\fR when the runs are compared; one modified after the run's
results were written is said to be, since it may hold another program's names.
.SS The escalation
\fBomn latency\fR pays for every detailed layer over the whole run. A session
with an \fBescalate:\fR block pays for one at a time: its cheap pass \(em
\fBsched.offcpu\fR for where the main thread blocked and in which state,
\fBcpu.oncpu_time\fR for what the process burned \(em is read every 200 ms,
and a state is named when it holds more than half of a reading and more than
any other. The main thread's time on a cpu is bounded both ways: no more than
the process burned, no more than what its own blocks left of the interval.
When the same state is named twice in a row, the run attaches that layer's held
requests and lets go of the ones it attached before \(em released first, so
two detailed layers are never paid for at once. Only \fBlatency\fR is
decided for; \fBtail\fR, \fBthroughput\fR and \fBenergy\fR are refused and
name the command that answers them.
.PP
Every held request's notes say when it was attached or let go and why, with
the reading that decided it; the cheap pass's notes carry the decisions. A
layer named that cannot be served \(em a source given to the program at exec,
which cannot be attached mid-run \(em is said with the reason, and the cheap
answer stands: nothing is attached in its place. Under \fB\-\-budget\fR, a
held request over its ceiling with nothing cheaper in its chain is let go
rather than ending the run, and says so; a request attached mid-run is weighed
once it has run for 100 ms, because before that its attach is most of its
account.
.SH THE VIEW
.B omn top
draws a run in ten views: \fBsystem\fR (the machine and its processes),
\fBflame\fR (on- and off-cpu stacks, and two runs' difference),
\fBledger\fR (what a process costs, per resource), \fBrequest\fR (one
request's life), \fBtimebar\fR (where the wall clock went, per thread),
\fBheatmap\fR (latency over time), \fBlanes\fR (threads and GPU streams
side by side), \fBwake\fR (who woke whom), \fBflows\fR (where the bytes
went) and \fBprocess\fR (one process at a glance). It is a projection,
never a collector: live, it asks for the sources its views need through the
same resolver and runner as
.BR "omn trace" ,
and a source this machine cannot serve is drawn as its reason where its panel
would be. Every figure is drawn in one trust style: dimmed where it is not to
be read at face value, with a badge saying why.
.PP
The first screen is \fBprocess\fR for a launched program or
\fB\-\-pid\fR, and \fBsystem\fR for the machine;
.BI \-\-view " name"
opens another.
.B \-\-replay
.I run
draws a recorded run (a directory written with
.BR \-o ,
its \fIresults.json\fR, or a flight recorder's document);
.B \-\-against
.I run
gives the views that compare a second one;
.B \-\-gallery
.I run
steps through every view on one run.
.B \-\-headless
draws without a terminal: a live run is collected to
.BR \-o ,
a replay is printed as text at
.BR \-\-size .
.B \-\-batch
prints the system view as a table a script parses, as
.B top \-b
does: live when the run ends, or a
.BR \-\-replay ;
shares are fractions, times nanoseconds, sizes bytes, and what a column is
not to be read at face value for is a
.B #
line.
.B \-\-sort
.I key
orders its tasks by cpu (the default), memory, faults, io or runqueue.
.B \-\-dump\-session
prints the session a live run would ask for.
.PP
Keys: \fBq\fR quits; \fB\(<-\fR/\fB\(->\fR or \fBtab\fR move between
views, \fB1\fR\(en\fB9\fR and \fB0\fR jump; \fBenter\fR goes into what
is selected \(em a process into its ledger, a ledger line into the view behind
it, a request into its threads' lanes \(em and \fBbackspace\fR comes back,
with the focus it had; \fBf\fR clears the focus; \fBM\fR turns the mouse
off and on; \fB?\fR lists every key and the trust legend.
.PP
The mouse: a click selects, a click on the selection goes in, the wheel
scrolls (or zooms where a view zooms), a right click comes back, a click on a
tab opens its view. Nothing is reachable by the mouse only. It is decoded in
SGR mode; inside
.BR tmux (1)
it needs
.B set \-g mouse on
to reach the view. While the mouse is captured, most terminals select text
with \fBshift\fR held, or \fBM\fR gives the selection back.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The measurement ran, or the question was answered.
.TP
.B 1
This run cannot happen here: this machine cannot serve the request, or
something failed while running. The last line of standard error says which,
in one of three fixed sentences a caller may match:
.RS
.EX
exit 1: the machine refused the run; nothing was measured
exit 1: the run failed after it started, before writing its results
exit 1: the run failed after it started; what it read is written above
.EE
.RE
.IP
The request itself is valid and will run on a
machine that has what it needs. Also a run that went to its end but whose
collector broke the program it measured \(em the GPU's injection library
killing its target as it exited \(em said in an
.B error:
line on standard error once the results are written: the program's own exit
status was then the tool's doing.
.TP
.B 2
The request was refused: it cannot mean what it asks for. A flag that does not
parse, an axis a source does not carry, a triage question without an objective
or with one its method does not answer for, a tail asked of one run.
.TP
.B 3
The request is valid and nothing in this build collects it yet.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OMN_CATALOG_DIR
Load the catalog from this directory instead of the one compiled in, and the
unwind modes from its
.I unwind.yaml
when it has one.
.TP
.B DEBUGINFOD_URLS
The debuginfod servers
.B \-\-symbols debuginfod
asks, space separated, in order. Unset or empty, that request is refused
before the target starts.
.TP
.B DEBUGINFOD_CACHE_PATH
Where fetched debug files are kept; else
.IR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/debuginfod_client ,
else
.IR ~/.cache/debuginfod_client ,
the cache elfutils, gdb and perf share.
.TP
.B OMN_PRELOAD_LIB
Path to the heap shim, when it is not beside the binary.
.TP
.B OMN_PROGRAM_STDERR
A file, created or truncated, that the launched program's standard error is
written to instead of the one it would share with
.BR omn ,
so the two are told apart at the source; what
.B \-\-program\-stderr
sets, which wins over it. The variable is
.BR omn 's
own and is not passed on to the program; a file that cannot be opened stops
the launch with the reason.
.TP
.B OMN_PROGRAM_STDOUT
The same for the launched program's standard output; what
.B \-\-program\-stdout
sets, which wins over it.
.TP
.B OMN_CONTAINER_RUNTIME
The one container runtime to ask for
.BR \-\-container ,
in place of
.BR docker ", " podman " and " nerdctl
in that order.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Set and non-empty, or
.B TERM=dumb
, disables colour.
.SH PRIVILEGES
The eBPF backends need
.B CAP_BPF
and
.BR CAP_PERFMON ,
and several sysfs counters need root. An unprivileged run is refused with the
capability that is missing, never resolved onto a lesser backend that answers a
different question.
.PP
File capabilities do not survive a relink, so every rebuild drops them and
nothing announces it: the tool reports fewer sources as available, which reads
like the machine changed rather than the binary.
.PP
.RS
.EX
sudo setcap cap_bpf,cap_perfmon+ep $(which omn)
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I catalog/*.yaml
The vocabulary, as data. Adding a source is an entry here, not code.
.TP
.I examples/*.yml
One session per question worth asking. Each opens with the question it answers
and what to expect.
.IR examples/triage\-*.yml " are the sessions the triage commands ask through."

.IR docs/GUIDE.md " explains what each one shows and how to run one, a family, or"
all of them.
.TP
.I <out>/resolved.json
The backend selected, every requirement and how it was probed, what was
degraded, and the machine's kernel, governor, THP mode, SMT state and
clocksource. Two measurements taken through different backends are not
comparable, and this is what says which was used.
.TP
.IR <out>/trigger\-N.<ext> ", " <out>/recording.json
A recording's documents, one per trigger, and its record: every trigger with
its window, why the run ended, and what was read after the last document and
is in none.
.TP
.I <out>/cost.json
What the measurement itself cost: the CPU time this tool burned, what the
collector spent inside the target
.RI ( in_target_cpu_ns ,
null when unknown, with
.I in_target_from
saying why), the CPU time the target burned over the same window and where
that number was read, what weighing the run cost, and the band the backend
declared set beside the percentage it actually came to.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Resident memory of a program, as it runs:
.EX
omn mem.rss \-\-emit timeline \-\-mode poll@100ms \-\- ./build.sh
.EE
.TP
Which allocation actually cost memory:
.EX
omn \-f examples/allocation\-cost.yml
.EE
.TP
Round\-trip time of every TCP connection this machine holds:
.EX
omn net.tcp.rtt \-\-system \-m poll@1s \-d 30s \-e timeline
.EE
.TP
Instructions retired by a program, the most reproducible number in the catalog:
.EX
omn cpu.instructions \-m count \-e summary \-\- ./bench
.EE
.TP
Where a program's cpu time goes, without naming a source, mode or emit:
.EX
omn cpu \-\- ./bench
omn sched \-\-cost med \-\- ./server \-\-once
.EE
.TP
The machine's energy while a program runs, as \fBperf stat \-a \-\- prog\fR:
.EX
omn power.energy \-\-system \-m count \-e summary \-\- ./bench
.EE
.TP
Two counters on one launch, five runs, with their spread:
.EX
omn cpu.instructions,cpu.branches \-m count \-e summary \-r 5 \-\- ./bench
.EE
.TP
A program's syscalls as \fBstrace \-c\fR's table, and each call as \fBstrace\fR prints it:
.EX
omn proc.syscall \-m count \-k syscall \-e summary \-\- ./server \-\-once
omn proc.calls \-e raw \-\-filter 'syscall == "openat"' \-\- ./server \-\-once
.EE
.TP
The machine's processes, as \fBtop \-b\fR lists them:
.EX
omn proc.tasks \-\-system \-m poll@1s \-e raw \-d 5s
.EE
.TP
A cycles profile, \fBperf record\fR's default:
.EX
omn cpu.cycles \-m sample@997hz \-e stack \-\- ./bench
.EE
.TP
What the machine counts as \fBnstat\fR, \fBss \-tuan\fR, \fBfree\fR and \fBvmstat\fR do:
.EX
omn net.snmp \-\-system \-m count \-e raw \-d 5s
omn net.sockets \-\-system \-m snapshot \-e raw
omn mem.system \-\-system \-m snapshot \-e summary
omn sched.interrupts,sched.runqueue \-\-system \-m poll@1s \-e timeline \-d 5s
omn net.snmp \-\-system \-m count \-k counter \-e summary \-\- ./client
omn net.sockstat \-\-system \-m snapshot \-e raw
.EE
.TP
What \fBslabtop\fR, \fBrunqlen\fR and \fBpowertop\fR print:
.EX
omn mem.slab \-\-system \-m snapshot \-e raw
omn sched.runqlen \-\-system \-m sample \-k cpu \-e hist \-d 5s
omn power.wakeups \-\-system \-m poll@1s \-k comm \-e summary \-d 5s
.EE
.TP
Which tasks the OOM killer chose, and what each held:
.EX
omn mem.oom \-\-system \-m trace \-e raw \-d 1h
.EE
.TP
Each time slice of a program, as \fBperf sched timehist\fR:
.EX
omn sched.slices \-e raw \-\- ./server \-\-once
.EE
.TP
Block I/O over 4 KiB, as a histogram:
.EX
omn io.latency \-\-system \-m trace \-e hist \-\-filter "bytes > 4KiB"
.EE
.TP
What this machine can serve, and what this build can measure:
.EX
omn list
.EE
.TP
Why a program is slow, for its median run:
.EX
omn latency \-\-objective latency \-\- ./server \-\-once
.EE
.TP
What makes its slowest run of twenty slow:
.EX
omn latency \-\-objective tail \-\-runs 20 \-\- ./server \-\-once
.EE
.TP
What a program queues for, and what it costs in energy:
.EX
omn saturate \-\-objective throughput \-\- ./build.sh
omn saturate \-\-objective energy \-\- ./build.sh
.EE
.TP
A run that decides which layer to pay for, from its own cheap numbers:
.EX
omn \-f examples/attach\-escalate\-phases.yml \-\-budget 5%
.EE
.TP
A flight recorder: a document each time a program's descriptors pass 150:
.EX
omn \-f examples/record\-descriptor\-bursts.yml \-o /tmp/bursts
omn proc.fd \-m poll@20ms \-e summary \-d 1h \-\-until 'value >= 150' \\
    \-\-retain 1s \-\-documents 5 \-\-pid 4242
.EE
.TP
Which function a change made slower:
.EX
omn cpu.profile \-m sample@997hz \-e stack \-\-stack fp \-\-format json \-o before \-\- ./old
omn cpu.profile \-m sample@997hz \-e stack \-\-stack fp \-\-format json \-o after \-\- ./new
omn diff before after
.EE
.TP
A join applied after the run, to what the run stored:
.EX
omn \-f session.yml \-\-format json \-o run
omn join \-f session.yml run
.EE
.TP
An LTTng-UST trace of the same program, joined with the kernel's switches:
.EX
omn sched.switches \-m trace \-k task \-e raw \-\-format json \-o run \-\- ./app
omn import ~/lttng\-traces/app\-session run \-o merged.json
omn join \-f rules.yml merged.json
.EE
.TP
Write a session down for a machine other than this one:
.EX
omn io.latency \-\-pid 1 \-m trace \-e hist \-\-no\-resolve \-\-dump\-request \-\-format yaml
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
